WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.400
Okay, welcome everyone.

00:02.400 --> 00:05.560
In today's lecture, we'll look into aspects of robot control.

00:05.560 --> 00:10.400
So this means at different levels of our autonomous robot, there are different kinds of controls

00:10.400 --> 00:11.400
needed.

00:11.400 --> 00:14.600
So at the trajectory level, we need to ensure that we are following the trajectory.

00:14.600 --> 00:18.360
At the position level, we need to ensure that we reach the point on the trajectory that

00:18.360 --> 00:19.580
we want.

00:19.580 --> 00:23.000
And even at a motor level, we need certain controls.

00:23.000 --> 00:26.440
And we look at all these aspects at these different levels.

00:26.440 --> 00:31.880
What we'll also look into is the popular controller called as the PID control, which is Proportional

00:31.880 --> 00:33.720
Integrative and Derivative Control.

00:33.720 --> 00:39.080
So this is a concept coming from the feedback control kind of controllers, and we'll see

00:39.080 --> 00:46.200
how we can use these PID controls for realizing the task of driving our robot along the desired

00:46.200 --> 00:47.500
trajectory.

00:47.500 --> 00:52.600
And finally, we'll also see some more advanced methods which are out there, which can give

00:52.600 --> 00:55.200
us some specific performance if you're looking for them.

00:55.200 --> 01:00.320
For example, if we want our system to be robust to, let's say, noise in our measurements,

01:00.320 --> 01:05.360
to noise in our state estimation and so on, we would need some specific kind of algorithms.

01:05.360 --> 01:10.280
If we want it to be failsafe, so for example, some part fails and we want it still to work,

01:10.280 --> 01:15.060
we'll also look at these kind of algorithms later on towards the end of the lecture.

01:15.060 --> 01:19.940
So before I get started, I just want to show you a rough control architecture that one

01:19.940 --> 01:23.860
sees when you have an autonomous robot in question.

01:23.860 --> 01:29.580
So what you see here in the gray box here, you could say this is the thinking part or

01:29.580 --> 01:33.380
the representation that the robot has of the world.

01:33.380 --> 01:37.420
And what we've seen in the other lectures are techniques, for example, to create a map

01:37.420 --> 01:39.740
of the environment using a sensor.

01:39.740 --> 01:44.340
So this could be a laser scanner, and you go around your room and create a map.

01:44.340 --> 01:48.260
So this is one aspect of, let's say, the representation of the world.

01:48.260 --> 01:50.920
And then you have the modules doing localization.

01:50.920 --> 01:56.480
So given this map, can we figure out where we are using then again some other sensors,

01:56.480 --> 02:00.160
for example, like laser scanner or GPS, et cetera.

02:00.160 --> 02:05.520
So all this forms, so these parts we've seen in the other lecture, and these, so to say,

02:05.520 --> 02:08.120
form the representation of the robot.

02:08.120 --> 02:13.360
And the way the robot usually interacts with the world, so the world here, is usually through

02:13.360 --> 02:14.800
the actuators.

02:14.800 --> 02:18.660
So if you have a mobile robot, these actuators could be wheels.

02:18.660 --> 02:23.020
So you have some motors attached to the wheels, which cause the movement and allow your platform

02:23.020 --> 02:24.460
to move in the environment.

02:24.460 --> 02:30.340
Whereas on the other end, if you want to perceive how you actually move, you equip your robot

02:30.340 --> 02:31.700
with some kind of sensors.

02:31.700 --> 02:37.720
So this could be wheel encoders, this could be GPS devices, laser scanners, whatever.

02:37.720 --> 02:42.860
So you have a representation of the world and yourself, so the thing in the gray box

02:42.860 --> 02:43.860
here.

02:43.860 --> 02:48.420
Then you have a way to actually move in the physical world using your actuators, so the

02:49.180 --> 02:55.220
And you have a way to actually sense what movement that you actually made.

02:55.220 --> 03:00.980
And using these three structures here, or three ideas, you can control the robot to

03:00.980 --> 03:04.300
go along a particular trajectory, for example.

03:04.300 --> 03:10.060
And the task that we will be specifically focusing in this lecture is what sits on this

03:10.060 --> 03:12.740
side of this graph here.

03:12.740 --> 03:15.900
So what we will look is three levels of control.

03:15.900 --> 03:20.340
At the lowest level, we'll first see how to actually control the motor itself.

03:20.340 --> 03:25.020
So if you want the motor to rotate at a particular speed, so in this case, let's say we talk

03:25.020 --> 03:27.420
about an autonomous car.

03:27.420 --> 03:31.300
This would mean that you would need to control the speed that you move at, and this would

03:31.300 --> 03:35.620
in turn mean that you would need to control the motors that you have.

03:35.620 --> 03:39.880
Then one level up would be to do some kind of a position control.

03:39.880 --> 03:42.700
This means that you want to reach a particular position in the environment.

03:42.700 --> 03:46.540
So there's some task you need to complete, and for this you need to go to a particular

03:46.540 --> 03:47.540
position.

03:47.540 --> 03:50.220
And we have some control at this level.

03:50.220 --> 03:54.540
And then finally, the higher level control would be a trajectory level control.

03:54.540 --> 04:01.180
So think of a situation where you have a map, and you have to go from your current position

04:01.180 --> 04:07.020
to a particular position, a destination position, and this would be, let's say your planning

04:07.020 --> 04:09.340
algorithm gives this plan.

04:09.340 --> 04:13.500
Then from this plan, you can generate some trajectories, so how to actually reach this

04:13.500 --> 04:15.740
position in a smooth manner.

04:15.740 --> 04:21.060
And once you have that, so how do you actually link all these three levels of control to

04:21.060 --> 04:23.020
achieve your task?

04:23.020 --> 04:29.580
And primarily, we will look at these three tasks today, and we'll always give the example

04:29.580 --> 04:35.380
of a mobile robot, say an autonomous car in an urban environment.

04:35.380 --> 04:40.660
But the idea kind of remains the same, whether it's a drone, a flying robot, or whether it's

04:40.660 --> 04:41.660
a humanoid.

04:41.660 --> 04:45.340
So this structure usually remains the same, and sometimes in the literature you would

04:45.340 --> 04:50.440
find that this structure is also called as the think, act, and sense paradigm.

04:50.440 --> 04:55.500
So this is the thinking part, this is the acting part, and this is the sensing part.

04:55.500 --> 05:00.580
So this was a motivation for where really the control fits in the overall autonomous

05:00.580 --> 05:07.020
system architecture, so to say, and to motivate why we really need to solve this task.

05:07.020 --> 05:11.460
So let's start at the lowest level, so at the level of the motor control.

05:11.460 --> 05:16.500
And for this, I give a really simple example, the example of a DC motor.

05:16.500 --> 05:20.840
So I guess most of you probably have seen this kind of motor in your school, or probably

05:20.840 --> 05:23.040
you even have built one.

05:23.040 --> 05:25.340
The idea, for example, here is quite simple.

05:25.340 --> 05:33.140
So you have two magnets, shown here by the north and the south pole, and the main idea

05:33.140 --> 05:39.100
here is when you have some electricity, some current flowing through a magnetic field,

05:39.100 --> 05:44.560
this particular wire which carries your current will experience some force.

05:44.560 --> 05:50.340
And that exactly happens here, so this is the diagram for a simple DC motor.

05:50.340 --> 05:56.060
So what you see here in gold here is basically some brushes which then connect to a coil

05:56.060 --> 05:59.100
which is going inside your magnetic field.

05:59.100 --> 06:04.880
And then what one does is to connect these wires to a battery, then we complete the circuit,

06:04.880 --> 06:08.620
and there is a current flowing through the coil.

06:08.620 --> 06:13.180
As we have the permanent magnets here, so we have a north and a south pole, so we have

06:13.180 --> 06:17.540
some magnetic field in this direction, and we have an electric field in the perpendicular

06:17.540 --> 06:18.600
direction.

06:18.600 --> 06:25.080
What this then does is to create a force on this shaft, moving it 180 degrees.

06:25.080 --> 06:30.880
So this way we have converted our electrical energy, so from the battery power, into mechanical

06:30.880 --> 06:32.700
energy of rotation.

06:32.700 --> 06:38.040
So you could also think of a motor as a transducer which converts one kind of energy into another

06:38.040 --> 06:39.360
kind of energy.

06:39.360 --> 06:46.100
So I give just these details to tell you that when we design a controller, it really goes

06:46.100 --> 06:51.180
down to quite a low level of understanding how the actuators work themselves.

06:51.180 --> 06:54.340
And in this case it's a simple DC motor.

06:54.340 --> 06:57.580
So now we have the actuator, which is basically a DC motor.

06:57.580 --> 07:01.300
The next question in terms of control is how can I control this?

07:01.300 --> 07:06.260
So for example for a motor, I would like it to move at a certain speed, so I would like

07:06.260 --> 07:11.660
to rotate it let's say 50 times per second if I want to go at a particular speed, then

07:11.660 --> 07:15.640
I would like to go at 100 rotations per second if I want to go faster.

07:15.640 --> 07:18.560
And the question is how can I actually control this?

07:18.560 --> 07:22.260
And for the DC motor this happens to be quite straightforward.

07:22.260 --> 07:27.540
So for the DC motor, essentially the idea is the more power you give, the more electricity

07:27.540 --> 07:30.220
you put in, the faster the thing would rotate.

07:30.220 --> 07:37.280
So the force on the coil here is directly proportional to the current that it's carrying.

07:37.280 --> 07:43.800
And therefore one way to actually do it is what is also called as pulse width modulation.

07:43.800 --> 07:51.320
So what you see here is a pulse, and here for example, usually the 0 volt is called

07:51.320 --> 07:55.000
the off state and the 5 volt is the on state.

07:55.000 --> 08:00.980
And at the extreme, if we keep it on for the entire duration of the pulse, this could correspond

08:00.980 --> 08:04.680
to the maximum speed at which the motor can rotate.

08:04.680 --> 08:09.280
And therefore to control the motor, so if you want to go at just let's say one fourth

08:09.280 --> 08:13.840
the speed, we would keep this signal on only for one fourth of the time.

08:13.840 --> 08:18.160
So there's a direct proportionality between the duty cycle, so the amount of time for

08:18.160 --> 08:25.720
which your current is on or the power is on, versus the overall period of the signal.

08:25.720 --> 08:30.420
So this way of control is called as pulse width modulation, and it's one of the most

08:30.420 --> 08:34.140
popular ways to control the DC motor.

08:34.140 --> 08:39.340
And basically you can buy chips like these which actually do this for you.

08:39.340 --> 08:43.760
So in this case actually there are four of these controllers, so you can control four

08:43.760 --> 08:46.100
such motors using a chip like this.

08:46.100 --> 08:52.420
So this is all the hobby, robotics, or even most of the professional ones would have some

08:52.420 --> 08:58.580
board like this which would then control the speed at which you can rotate the motors.

08:58.580 --> 09:05.180
So in an ideal world if our motors were perfect, if what we ask it to perform it does, then

09:05.180 --> 09:06.180
we are done.

09:06.180 --> 09:10.140
So we don't need any more theories, so to say.

09:10.140 --> 09:15.600
But in practice what we see is that such open loop controls in the sense what I just said,

09:15.600 --> 09:22.300
so you ask the motor to let's say rotate at 50 revolutions per second, 50 rotations per

09:22.300 --> 09:23.300
second.

09:23.300 --> 09:28.940
It might give you just 48, or it might give you 52, because some things are not calibrated

09:28.940 --> 09:34.880
well, maybe you don't have a direct relationship between the current that you give to the rotations

09:34.880 --> 09:38.980
that you give which is perfect, so there's some things which are imperfect there.

09:38.980 --> 09:42.600
And this kind of control is also called as an open loop control in the sense that you

09:42.600 --> 09:47.540
give a command, the controller executes it, and you have no knowledge what happens.

09:47.540 --> 09:50.720
So here comes this idea of a feedback control.

09:50.720 --> 09:55.600
So usually most systems what they do is that you have the controller to which you give

09:55.600 --> 10:00.360
the desired value, so in this case this desired value could be the number of rotations per

10:00.360 --> 10:01.360
minute.

10:01.360 --> 10:06.480
So say you want 50 rotations per minute, then you give your control, you ask this guy to

10:06.480 --> 10:11.520
produce certain electricity, it does this, and then moves the system.

10:11.520 --> 10:16.080
So once you move the system, then you can observe this movement through a sensor.

10:16.080 --> 10:19.200
So in case of a motor this is usually an encoder.

10:19.200 --> 10:23.560
You can attach to the shaft of the motor some kind of an encoder and this tells you how

10:23.560 --> 10:26.680
many revolutions that you actually made.

10:26.680 --> 10:32.200
So now for example like I said before, you observe that you have only made 48 revolutions.

10:32.200 --> 10:36.120
And you can take this different signal between what you want and between what you actually

10:36.120 --> 10:37.120
observe.

10:37.120 --> 10:43.200
So let's say 50 minus 48 which is 2, so you are missing 2 more rotations per minute.

10:43.200 --> 10:47.880
And then this signal, the controller should be smart enough such that it takes this error

10:47.880 --> 10:53.720
signal, so this 2 revolutions per minute signal, and then generate more power so to

10:53.720 --> 10:59.200
say to compensate for the fact that you are not moving as fast as you think.

10:59.200 --> 11:05.360
So in general this kind of scheme is called as a feedback control where you also sense

11:05.360 --> 11:13.920
actually how the system is evolving and use this information to recompute your controls.

11:13.920 --> 11:19.880
So in a way this is a smarter way than let's say an open loop controller where you can

11:19.880 --> 11:22.760
have much more noise.

11:22.760 --> 11:28.240
And actually this idea is not something new, and this problem is something which we solve

11:28.240 --> 11:30.680
every day in our shower in the morning.

11:30.680 --> 11:37.080
So if you think of this feedback example, so let's say I like to have my shower at 35

11:37.080 --> 11:42.760
degrees, and when I enter the shower the water is at 30 degrees.

11:42.760 --> 11:51.080
So to say 35 is my desired state, 30 degrees is my current state of the water.

11:51.080 --> 11:55.440
Then the control that I have for the shower is this knob here, so you can move it left

11:55.440 --> 12:00.560
or right, you can rotate it left or right, and then this adds let's say more cold water

12:00.560 --> 12:05.440
or more hot water to actually change the water temperature.

12:05.440 --> 12:10.240
Then you stand under the shower and then in this case you feel the water fall on you and

12:10.240 --> 12:14.400
then you could say your skin, so to say, is the sensor which tells you okay, is this

12:14.400 --> 12:19.920
warm enough, is this too hot, is this too cold, and then you use this information, so

12:19.920 --> 12:25.200
what your skin tells you or what your body tells you, and then you say okay you have

12:25.200 --> 12:32.280
an idea of okay now the water seems to be 32 instead of 30 and it's still not 35 so

12:32.280 --> 12:37.080
I still need to move the lever more to the left because left here let's say adds more

12:37.080 --> 12:38.160
hot water.

12:38.160 --> 12:44.120
So this is a problem that we solve every day and it's the same with the robot control

12:44.120 --> 12:45.120
as well.

12:45.120 --> 12:49.640
So we want the signal to reach or we want the system to reach a particular state and

12:49.640 --> 12:53.720
we keep observing what the state is or at least what the measurable part of the state

12:53.720 --> 12:57.760
is and use it to drive our system.

12:57.760 --> 13:03.480
So if you want to see kind of the same system more mathematically this is what we show here.

13:03.480 --> 13:09.760
So the controller is usually defined as a function of your error function, so you have

13:09.760 --> 13:11.320
your error signal.

13:11.320 --> 13:16.560
So the input to the controller is an error signal which is basically the desired value

13:16.560 --> 13:19.980
for your state minus what you actually measure.

13:19.980 --> 13:24.880
So if your desired value is X test and what you measure is Zt it's a difference of this.

13:24.880 --> 13:31.440
So technically it might also happen that the value that you measure may not be the entire

13:31.440 --> 13:32.440
state.

13:32.440 --> 13:37.560
So for example of a 2D robot for example let's say your 2D robot has the state is defined

13:37.560 --> 13:40.360
by your XY position in the orientation.

13:40.360 --> 13:44.640
However you have a sensor say like the GPS sensor which only gives you the position and

13:44.640 --> 13:46.600
no orientation information.

13:46.600 --> 13:51.540
This means that your sensor cannot observe the orientation of the robot.

13:51.540 --> 13:55.740
So no matter what orientation it is in you don't really see it.

13:55.740 --> 14:00.960
In our examples we will see a simple case where we say that we observe all the states.

14:00.960 --> 14:05.320
However in practice this is something we should remember that not all the states that

14:05.320 --> 14:08.360
you want to track may be observable.

14:08.360 --> 14:13.240
So we saw the controller so the goal of this lecture is actually to design this guy such

14:13.240 --> 14:19.080
that given an error signal it produces a UT which is a control to your system.

14:19.080 --> 14:24.200
So this could be how fast your motor moves or go to a certain position in the environment

14:24.200 --> 14:27.140
and along the trajectory and so on.

14:27.140 --> 14:30.200
And then we have a system definition.

14:30.200 --> 14:33.260
Usually we have a model of how the system evolves.

14:33.260 --> 14:38.000
So in this case for example usually we model them in a linear way.

14:38.000 --> 14:43.800
So the system at time t depends on what the system state or what the state is at time

14:43.800 --> 14:48.100
t minus 1 plus some controls that you give to it.

14:48.100 --> 14:51.960
So this is a very simple simplified formula here.

14:51.960 --> 14:56.620
In principle you could have more nonlinear functions which map your current state to

14:56.620 --> 14:57.820
the future state.

14:57.820 --> 15:01.060
In this case let's say this works well for the shower case where you say the current

15:01.060 --> 15:06.980
temperature of the water is the previous temperature plus the amount of let's say the new hot water

15:06.980 --> 15:08.320
that you add in.

15:08.320 --> 15:12.580
And so for such a system this is good enough but for many mobile robotic systems we'll

15:12.580 --> 15:16.460
see that we'll need something more complicated.

15:16.460 --> 15:22.780
And what we should also consider that this system we don't always know perfectly so it's

15:22.780 --> 15:25.580
our assumption how this system works.

15:25.580 --> 15:31.060
So there's some simplifications we make just for modeling purposes and this also causes

15:31.060 --> 15:34.540
some error in our estimation problem.

15:34.540 --> 15:38.980
So in the sense that we predict our system to be in a particular place but this might

15:38.980 --> 15:43.600
not be the case because our model is too simplistic.

15:43.600 --> 15:47.480
So this is a rough block diagram for all the problems that we'll see essentially they're

15:47.480 --> 15:50.480
of this kind today.

15:50.480 --> 15:53.280
And let's say we want to design the simplest controller.

15:53.280 --> 15:57.340
So what would be one of the simplest controller that we could design?

15:57.340 --> 16:03.760
Say this let's say we start with what is called as the proportional control in which the idea

16:03.760 --> 16:12.780
is just that your control law so ut is just a proportion so it's a constant times your

16:12.780 --> 16:13.860
error signal.

16:13.860 --> 16:18.920
So what this says is that if your error is high give a high input if your error is low

16:18.920 --> 16:26.220
give a lower input multiplied by some constant so do I want to take some map directly the

16:26.220 --> 16:33.220
whole error as the control or do I want to do let's say 10% of it so I could have k as

16:33.220 --> 16:39.300
0.1 then this would mean take the error and just apply 0.1 of it and so on.

16:39.300 --> 16:45.020
So let's see basically let's see the example for the shower and see how this proportional

16:45.020 --> 16:49.120
control would work in different cases that could happen in reality.

16:49.120 --> 16:55.440
So this is the most ideal case here we take a kp as 1 so essentially our control law ut

16:55.440 --> 16:59.120
is nothing but one times the error function.

16:59.120 --> 17:05.680
So what you will see here also in the upcoming slides are basically two measurements one

17:05.680 --> 17:10.160
on the top here is the temperature so this is the actual state of the water or the temperature

17:10.160 --> 17:15.260
of the water that you are taking the shower in and on the bottom you see the control.

17:15.260 --> 17:22.300
So this control here we say this is basically the number of degrees in terms of temperature

17:22.300 --> 17:28.780
and not in terms of angles so let's say we map our handle so let's say if we move it

17:28.780 --> 17:35.980
half way around this represents whatever 5 degrees more heat adding more heat to the

17:35.980 --> 17:41.120
water and if we move it completely it would be 10 degrees and something like this.

17:41.120 --> 17:48.600
So we start at time t equal to 0 water here it shows 25 degrees and we want to reach a

17:48.600 --> 17:53.920
desired position of 35 degrees.

17:53.920 --> 18:00.640
Then what we do is we apply this control law so it says u is k times et and k is 1 so the

18:00.640 --> 18:06.800
error is basically 10 degrees we are at 25 we want to be at 35 the error is 10 and therefore

18:06.800 --> 18:12.740
our control would be applied 10 degrees more heat to the water and this is what we see

18:12.740 --> 18:18.900
so in one second we apply 10 degrees we reach our desired temperature.

18:18.900 --> 18:24.700
So here in the z you see what we actually observe in this case let's say we are able

18:24.700 --> 18:29.380
to observe the temperature which means this blue line is what we actually observe using

18:29.380 --> 18:35.280
a sensor whereas the red is actually the true temperature itself in practice we wouldn't

18:35.280 --> 18:41.320
know this so this is some state internal to the world and we can only estimate it we don't

18:41.320 --> 18:47.160
know it but in these graphs that you would see we assume to know this just to compare

18:47.160 --> 18:50.880
our measurements to what the actual state is.

18:50.880 --> 18:55.480
In this perfect ideal case no noise nothing this is perfect you apply one control you

18:55.480 --> 19:00.880
reach the state you want and it's done but you know from your experience in the shower

19:00.880 --> 19:04.620
that this is never the case you can't just move your handle once and you're happy with

19:04.620 --> 19:09.580
the water right why is this so one of the things is that there is certain amount of

19:09.580 --> 19:16.100
noise in the system you think that moving the handle by a certain amount should increase

19:16.100 --> 19:20.460
the water temperature by certain amount but there's always some noise so maybe there's

19:20.460 --> 19:27.000
some noise in the way the water is being mixed maybe there's some delay in the time

19:27.000 --> 19:33.640
between when you actually mix it and when it happens so one of these effects is the

19:33.640 --> 19:38.900
noise part and then maybe also it depends on your sensor so maybe you actually detect

19:38.900 --> 19:44.820
that the water is hotter or colder a bit later or you also detect it with some error so to

19:44.820 --> 19:49.840
say what I want to convey here that you can have system or measurement error so you can

19:49.840 --> 19:55.660
have errors in your model of how the thing works or you could also have some errors in

19:55.660 --> 20:00.280
your sensors and these are the two kinds of errors that you need to take care of and let's

20:00.280 --> 20:07.180
see what happens for example in the same case like before but now we have say noise in our

20:07.180 --> 20:12.620
measurements in this case so it could have been also in the system but we just show here

20:12.620 --> 20:18.620
on the measurements so basically this blue line here you see here has some noise and

20:18.640 --> 20:25.360
what does this do then so for example you start again at 0 you want to go to 35 and

20:25.360 --> 20:31.600
then you apply certain amount of different certain control but then you cross this 35

20:31.600 --> 20:37.160
